2. Discover — add a new “Lore” section

Current implementation

Discover currently contains three sections:

1. Fighters
2. Movement
3. Launch

The navigation is defined in js/screens/discover-screen.js, using the SECTIONS registry.

The page styling is in css/discover.css.

Required changes

Add a fourth section named Lore.

The new section order should be:

1. Fighters
2. Lore
3. Movement
4. Launch

Keep Fighters as the default section when entering Discover.

Lore page design

Lore should feel like a natural part of the existing Discover interface, not a completely different screen.

Create a dedicated Lore page with:

* A properly styled Lore heading.
* A clean, readable content area.
* Structured sections suitable for worldbuilding and character history.
* Comfortable text spacing and typography.
* Subtle separators and existing Alva colors.
* Vertical scrolling if its content exceeds the available height.

The page should be visually engaging but restrained.

Content requirements:

Lore is for narrative information, worldbuilding, character backgrounds, and established story context.

It must not become another gameplay guide or repeat the Movement and Launch sections.

Review the repository’s existing documentation for established narrative information before writing the initial content.

Do not invent major canonical events, character relationships, fictional factions, origins, or world history without a source in the project.

If there is not enough established lore to populate a complete page, create a polished introductory Lore section with concise placeholder or introductory copy indicating that more stories will be added. Prepare the structure for future lore entries.

Avoid confusing gameplay mechanics with confirmed fictional-world lore.

Future-proof the Lore system

Make future lore additions straightforward.

Ideally, keep Lore content separate from navigation logic, using a clear structured data source or localization keys where appropriate.

It should be possible to add future world lore or character stories without rewriting the Discover navigation.

Do not hardcode special behavior for character #0001 or #0002.

Navigation requirements

The Lore tab must behave exactly like the other Discover tabs.

* Mouse click activates it.
* Keyboard focus activates it.
* Gamepad navigation can reach it.
* Touch navigation works.
* Only one Discover section is active at a time.
* Hidden sections cannot receive focus.
* The active tab has the existing visual treatment.
* Screen readers receive the correct tab and panel relationships.
* The content is independently scrollable when needed.
* Back navigation remains unchanged.

Preserve the existing accessible tablist, tab, and tabpanel structure, including aria-selected, aria-controls, and roving tabindex behavior.

Responsive behavior

The new fourth tab must fit naturally into Discover’s layout.

On wide screens, maintain the vertical navigation rail.

On narrow screens, maintain the horizontal navigation rail.

Do not reduce labels to unreadable sizes or shrink touch targets below their existing minimum.

Allow the horizontal rail to scroll when four labels cannot fit, including when using French translations.

Avoid clipping, horizontal page overflow, or broken navigation in short landscape windows.

